  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1205 PM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nRest of Today - Sunday: Isolated Showers this Afternoon and Evening \n\nCooling temperatures aloft with northerly flow in place will \nallow for some instability (around 500-750 J/kg of MUCAPE) to be\npresent across the region this afternoon and evening allowing \nfor the development of some isolated showers during this \ntimeframe as hinted at in some of the CAMs. Overall, given the \nfairly skinny instability profiles and lack of deep-layer shear,\nwould think that robust convective development is very \nunlikely. In any case, cannot rule out (15-25% chance) some \nisolated showers late this afternoon and into the evening. \n\nOtherwise, upper-level ridging with accompanying surface high \npressure will move into the region overnight and into Sunday \nresulting in clearing skies and light winds near the surface. \nConsequently, cannot fully rule out some fog formation in river \nvalleys, however the stronger winds persisting off the surface will \nlikely mitigate this. Temperatures will likely remain seasonable \nwith northeasterly surface flow keeping temperatures slightly cooler \nthan normal with highs generally in the upper 70s to around 80 \ndegrees for Sunday. \n\nMonday - Wednesday: Increasing Rain Chances Tuesday/Wednesday\n\nFairly dry conditions are expected to begin the work week with the \naforementioned upper-level ridging and surface high pressure in \nplace for Monday. Consequently, a relatively persistence forecast is \nexpected for Monday with highs generally in the upper 70s to lower \n80s with minimal sky cover. However, the pattern changes as we head \ninto Tuesday as a shortwave trough pushes through the Dakotas by \nTuesday morning, switching our flow aloft to more zonal. As this \noccurs, a weak surface boundary associated with the aforementioned \nshortwave trough pushes through the area late Tuesday afternoon \nthrough Wednesday with the grand ensemble (GEFS/EC/Canadian \nensemble) having increased probabilities (50-80%) for measurable \nprecipitation during this period. Overall, not overly impressed with \ninstability and shear profiles in the deterministic GFS during this \nperiod as a fairly substantial low-level EML keeps sounding \nprofiles fairly capped. This limited potential for severe \nweather is highlighted in the joint probabilities for SBCAPE and\nbulk shear in the grand ensemble (GEFS/EC/Canadian ensemble) \nhaving extremely low (under 5%) for having favored shear and \ninstability during this period. Consequently, thinking that \nwhile this is our next period for widespread rain across the \narea, the probability for organized severe weather is very low \nat this time. \n\nThursday - Friday: Seasonable, Small Chances for Showers and Storms\n\nAs we head into the later half of next week, continued zonal to \nnorthwesterly mid-level flow will perpetuate the risk for any \nshowers and storms if any shortwave perturbations can sneak \ntheir way into the area. There are some probabilities (20-40%) \nin the grand ensemble (GEFS/EC/Canadian) for favorable \ninstability (over 1000 J/kg of SBCAPE) and bulk shear (over 30 \nkts) for severe weather across the area during this period. \nHowever, confidence across deterministic and ensemble guidance \nis fairly low with how these features would be resolved so \nthinking the small precipitation chances in the NBM (15-25%) are\nappropriate at this time. Additionally, with continued zonal to\nnorthwesterly flow, thinking temperatures will remain \nrelatively seasonable with the NBM keeping the inter-decile \nrange for high temperatures in the middle 70s to upper 80s \nthrough Friday.",
